<%# THE FIVE DOORS ONTO ONE DATED ROW (computed-claims spec §3.3). Local: `rule`
    (BudgetPagePresenter::Rule).

    THE USER'S WORDS AND NEVER THE TABLE'S. A rate rule's envelope is TOPPED UP or REDUCED for this
    period; an accruing rule's fund is SET ASIDE into, TAKEN BACK out of, or SKIPPED. Each of the
    five writes the same `(rule, date, signed amount)` row, and the word "adjustment" appears
    nowhere on this screen — it is the spec's word for the mechanism, not the app's word for the act.

    ONE FORM AND ONE AMOUNT FIELD, WITH THE DIRECTION ON THE BUTTON. Two forms would ask the user to
    read two identical inputs and choose one; a signed field would ask them to type a minus. A
    browser submits only the button that was pressed, so `amount_sign` carries the direction and
    `AdjustmentsController#amount_for` applies it to the magnitude.

    SKIP CARRIES NO FIGURE. It posts `skip=1` and the server computes −planned, because the planned
    share moves the moment another delta lands and a hidden field rendered before that would skip
    the wrong amount. Offered only where the period plans something (`Rule#skippable?`) — a fund
    already at its target plans nothing, and a skip there would be a zero row the model refuses.

    `<details>` RATHER THAN A STIMULUS CONTROLLER, the idiom `_suggestions` already uses on this
    page: the whole interaction is "open this", the browser has it, and there is no JavaScript here
    for a Turbo navigation to break.

    `draggable="false"` IS LOAD-BEARING. The `<section data-category-group>` this sits inside is
    `draggable="true"` for the reorder, and a text input inside a draggable ancestor loses its
    selection to the drag in Chrome — the whole panel opts out so the fields behave like fields. %>
<details class="mt-2" draggable="false" data-adjust="<%= budget_rule_name(rule.budget) %>">
  <summary class="text-sm text-brand-dark hover:text-brand cursor-pointer inline-block">Adjust</summary>

  <%# `max-w-xl` (design pass at 1440): the panel is two short fields and three buttons, and left to
      the row's full width it stretched to the far edge of a 1440px card with two-thirds of it
      empty — a form that looks like it is missing something. It still fills a 375px viewport. %>
  <div class="mt-2 max-w-xl rounded border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 px-3 py-3 space-y-3">
    <%# THE PLANNED FIGURE, ALWAYS SAID BEFORE IT IS CHANGED. On a rate rule it is what the envelope
        gets this period; on an accruing one it is what the fund is putting away this period, and it
        is also exactly what Skip takes back — so the button below can name its own amount. %>
    <p class="text-xs text-gray-600" data-adjust-planned>
      <%= number_to_currency(rule.planned_this_period) %>
      <%= rule.rate? ? "planned this period" : "going in this period" %>
    </p>

    <%= form_with url: adjustments_path, method: :post, class: "space-y-2" do %>
      <%= hidden_field_tag :rule_id, rule.budget.id %>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end gap-2">
        <%# IDS ARE KEYED ON THE RULE, because this partial renders once per row and a repeated `id`
            makes every label on the page point at the first row's input. %>
        <div>
          <%= label_tag "adjust-amount-#{rule.budget.id}", "How much", class: "block text-xs text-gray-600 mb-1" %>
          <%= number_field_tag :amount,
                               nil,
                               id: "adjust-amount-#{rule.budget.id}",
                               step: 0.01,
                               min: 0.01,
                               required: true,
                               placeholder: "0.00",
                               class: "form-input w-28" %>
        </div>

        <%# LEFT BLANK ON PURPOSE, and the hint says so. "Today" has exactly one spelling in this
            app — the controller's, read in the owner's zone — and a value rendered here would be a
            second one, free to disagree with it on a page left open overnight. %>
        <div>
          <%= label_tag "adjust-date-#{rule.budget.id}", "On", class: "block text-xs text-gray-600 mb-1" %>
          <%= date_field_tag :date, nil, id: "adjust-date-#{rule.budget.id}", class: "form-input" %>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <%= button_tag rule.rate? ? "Top up this period" : "Set aside",
                       type: :submit,
                       name: "amount_sign",
                       value: "1",
                       class: "btn btn-primary" %>
        <%= button_tag rule.rate? ? "Reduce this period" : "Take back",
                       type: :submit,
                       name: "amount_sign",
                       value: "-1",
                       class: "btn btn-secondary" %>
      </div>

      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">Today unless you pick a day. It only changes this period.</p>
    <% end %>

    <% if rule.skippable? %>
      <%= form_with url: adjustments_path, method: :post do %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag :rule_id, rule.budget.id %>
        <%= hidden_field_tag :skip, "1" %>
        <%= button_tag "Skip this period (−#{number_to_currency(rule.planned_this_period)})",
                       type: :submit,
                       class: "btn btn-secondary",
                       data: { adjust_skip: true } %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</details>
